# The oracle image, pinned.
#
# One line per reference, tab-separated: <name> <image-ref> <version> <what it
# answers>. Read by tools/oracle/oracle_env.py, which splits on tabs and takes
# three fields. It is machine-read, not documentation.
#
# All five names share one image, which is the shape this library needs and not
# the shape `unicode` and `font` have: a single `make test` reaches for several
# of these references, sometimes in one test binary, so five images would be
# five things to keep in step for no gain. notes/suite/CONTAINERS.md section 4a
# is the argument for having an image here at all - the problem it solves is
# *absence*, not drift. Every reference below is present on the machine this
# was written on, which is why twenty-one tests that reach for one have never
# yet failed to find it, and why the skip they would print reads as success.
#
# Two kinds of pin and they are not the same guarantee (CONTAINERS.md section
# 2.6). This image is **built here**, so there is no digest to trust: what is
# pinned is everything its Containerfile reads, and the run-time version check
# is the real guarantee. `oracle_env.check_pin()` therefore runs in **both**
# modes, as in `font` and unlike `unicode`, because no line below names a stock
# image whose digest could stand in for the check.
#
# Raising a pin is deliberate. A newer Pillow is newer answers, and a new
# answer that disagrees is a finding to triage rather than a regression to fix.
#
# Building it:
#   podman build -t ghoti-image-oracle-refs:deb13-2 \
#       -f tools/oracle/containers/image-refs/Containerfile tools/oracle
# `make oracle-build` does exactly that. The tag says deb13 because the base is
# what decides four of the five versions, and -1 is a revision that moves when
# the contents do; `:latest` is never a tag here.

# The three decoders that come from Debian at a pinned version.
#
# libjpeg-turbo is reached two ways - through Pillow and through
# tests/tools/jpeg-oracle - and inside this image both spellings are the same
# 2.1.5, which is a change from the host where Pillow's pip wheel bundles its
# own. jpeg_test_utils.cpp tries Pillow first and the dumper otherwise; that
# fallback can no longer change the answer.
pillow	localhost/ghoti-image-oracle-refs:deb13-2	Pillow 11.1.0	Decoded pixels for JPEG, and the reader every verify_*_output.py checks our output with
libjpeg	localhost/ghoti-image-oracle-refs:deb13-2	libjpeg-turbo 2.1.5	Decoded pixels and DCT coefficients, through tests/tools/jpeg-oracle
giflib	localhost/ghoti-image-oracle-refs:deb13-2	giflib 5.2.2	LZW expansion, interlace order, colour tables and geometry - not compositing

# The other two BMP decoders tests/data/bmp/bmpsuite_sweep.py corroborates
# with. They arrived a tag after the rest, because `make bmpsuite` is not part
# of `make test` and so nothing in a routine build had ever asked them
# anything - which is the same blindness in a different place: the survey that
# found twenty-one skipped comparisons was reading the test binaries, and this
# target is not one.
pixbuf	localhost/ghoti-image-oracle-refs:deb13-2	GdkPixbuf 2.42.12	A third reading of every BMP in the suite
netpbm	localhost/ghoti-image-oracle-refs:deb13-2	netpbm 11.10.2	A fourth, through bmptopnm

# The two that are a git checkout rather than a release. Their version field is
# `@VERSIONS`, which means the pin lives in tools/oracle/VERSIONS and is read
# from there.
#
# Deliberately an indirection rather than a copy. Those two commits were pinned
# long before this image existed, VERSIONS says why in its own words, and
# fetch.sh reads it with `awk '$1 == name { print $2; exit }'` - first match
# wins. CONTAINERS.md section 2.2: a pin written in two files is a pin that can
# disagree with itself, and the disagreement is silent.
#
# What the image adds to them is the other half of the pin. A commit says which
# *source*; it does not say which compiler built it. "bmplib agrees on 79 of 91
# files" named a decoder built by whatever the host had until now.
bmplib	localhost/ghoti-image-oracle-refs:deb13-2	@VERSIONS	OS/2 Huffman 1D, bitmap arrays and 64-bit BMPs - the formats no installed decoder reads
bmpsuite	localhost/ghoti-image-oracle-refs:deb13-2	@VERSIONS	The 91-file conformance corpus tests/data/bmp/bmpsuite_sweep.py runs

# Not here, and each is its own piece of work:
#   libpng      a second reading of every PNG this library writes; Pillow is
#               the reader today and it cannot see bKGD at all, so the gate it
#               anchors is narrower than it looks (memory: png-bkgd-oracles)
#   IJG v10     third_party/jpeg-10, built by hand from a tarball today, and
#               the only reference here for SOF3 lossless and for arithmetic
#               coding. It is the one this library most wants pinned next.
